.039 Tc 0 Tw (thousand people. In addition, almost every country in the world closed )Tj320 0 TD 0 Tc 2.031 Tw (their gates to the Jews)Tj-320 -12 TD 0.061 Tc 0 Tw (thus trapping them in Nazi-occupied Europe.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc (The second theme was )Tj102 0 TD 1.557 Tw (the encouragement to be offered to the Arabs to leave Palestine.)Tj-128 -12 TD 0.143 Tc 0 Tw ("Here too, in Palestine surely is a )Tj153 0 TD 0 Tc 0.666 Tw (case, on human grounds and to promote a stable settlement,)Tj-153 -12 TD 0 Tw (for transfer )Tj53 0 TD 0.861 Tw (of population. Let the Arabs be encouraged to move out, as the Jews move in. Let)Tj-53 -12 TD 3.866 Tw (them be compensated handsomely for their land and let their settlement elsewhere )Tj415 0 TD 0 Tw (be)Tj-415 -12 TD 0.062 Tc (carefully organised and generously financed.")Tj26 -12 TD 0.16 Tc (The third and final theme dealt with the extension of the boundaries of )Tj327 0 TD 0 Tc 6.79 Tw (Palestine. "The)Tj-353 -12 TD 0.128 Tc 0 Tw (Arabs have many wide territories of )Tj167 0 TD 0 Tc 1.064 Tw (their own; they must not claim to exclude the Jews from)Tj-167 -12 TD 0.454 Tc 0 Tw (this )Tj21 0 TD 0 Tc 2.039 Tw (small area of Palestine, less than the size of Wales. Indeed we should re-examine also)Tj-21 -12 TD 2.632 Tw (the possibility of )Tj84 0 TD 2.723 Tw (extending the present Palestinian boundaries, by agreement with Egypt,)Tj-84 -12 TD 0.128 Tc 0 Tw (Syria or Transjordan." \()Tj/F4 7 Tf106 3 TD (2)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj/F3 10 Tf-110 -24 TD 0.095 Tc (How the Palestine Paragraph was Formulated)Tj/F4 10 Tf26 -12 TD 0 Tc (The )Tj20 0 TD 1.236 Tw (author of this report, Hugh Dalton, was born in 1887 and educated at Eton, King's)Tj-46 -12 TD 1.931 Tw (College Cambridge and the London School of )Tj218 0 TD 2.031 Tw (Economics. After unsuccessfully contesting a)Tj-218 -12 TD 0.05 Tc 0 Tw (number of Parliamentary seats between 1922-4, he )Tj227 0 TD 0 Tc 0.735 Tw (was elected as a Labour member in 1924. In)Tj-227 -12 TD 0.065 Tc 0 Tw (1931, he was defeated but was re-elected in 1935. In 1940, he )Tj272 0 TD 0 Tc 1.201 Tw (was made a member of the Privy)Tj-272 -12 TD -0.034 Tc 0 Tw (Council - a body consisting of several hundred distinguished men )Tj292 0 TD 0 Tc 2.023 Tw (drawn from all walks of life)Tj-292 -12 TD 1.411 Tw (whose function is to give private advice to the Sovereign. Dalton was also a member )Tj396 0 TD 3.839 Tw (of the)Tj-396 -12 TD 0.173 Tc 0 Tw (Parliamentary Committee of the Parliamentary Labour )Tj254 0 TD 0 Tc 1.971 Tw (Party intermittently from 1925 and a)Tj-254 -12 TD 0.061 Tc 0 Tw (member of the National Executive Committee of the Labour Party from 1926.)Tj26 -12 TD 0.181 Tc (Already in May 1943, Dalton was involved in the \322Post-War Aims of the British )Tj368 0 TD -0.18 Tc (Labour)TjET0.24 w 85.12 131.88 m 228.12 131.88 l S 1 w BT/F4 7 Tf85.5 119 TD (1)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0 Tc 1.458 Tw ( / The International Post-War Settlement, \(London, 1944\) ; )Tj221 0 TD 2.482 Tw (Report of the Forty-third Annual Conference of the)Tj-225 -9 TD 0.023 Tc 0 Tw (Labour Party, London, \(London, 1944\), pp.4-9.)Tj/F4 7 Tf0 -7 TD (2)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0 Tc ( / Ibid., p.7 ; Ibid., p.9.)Tj/F5 7 Tf172 -31 TD 0.269 Tc (\321    248    \321)TjET
