BT/F2 7 Tf181.5 781 TD 0.023 Tc 0 Tw (Chaim SIMONS : Proposals to Transfer Arabs from Palestine)Tj-10 -10 TD -0.18 Tc (\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321)Tj/F4 10 Tf-86 -25 TD 0.197 Tc (Arabs to live in a Jewish state.)Tj/F3 10 Tf0 -24 TD -0.214 Tc (Permanent Mandates Commission)Tj/F4 10 Tf26 -12 TD 0.106 Tc (The year 1920 saw the creation of )Tj153 0 TD 0 Tc 0.73 Tw (the Permanent Mandates Commission, whose function)Tj-179 -12 TD 4.253 Tw (was to examine the reports of the various Mandatory Powers )Tj315 0 TD 5.171 Tw (and present facts and)Tj-315 -12 TD 0 Tw (recommendations to the Council of the League of Nations.)Tj26 -12 TD (Its )Tj14 0 TD 2.082 Tw (thirty-second \(extraordinary\) session, held in Geneva, Switzerland between 30 July)Tj-40 -12 TD 2.901 Tw (and 18 August 1937, was devoted entirely to )Tj222 0 TD 5.47 Tw (Palestine. As in the case of the British)Tj-222 -12 TD 4.815 Tw (Parliamentary debates, the discussions of the Mandates Commission revealed a )Tj402 0 TD 0 Tw (wide)Tj-402 -12 TD 0.08 Tc (divergence of opinion on the validity of )Tj181 0 TD 0 Tc 1.314 Tw (the conclusions of the Peel Commission, in particular)Tj-181 -12 TD 0.243 Tc 0 Tw (with reference to the workability of the Mandate and the desirability of partition.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 2.948 Tw (The British member )Tj99 0 TD 3.394 Tw (of the Permanent Mandates Commission, Lord Hailey, was in)Tj-125 -12 TD 1.597 Tw (favour of the Peel )Tj87 0 TD 2.368 Tw (plan. He considered that a sound scheme of partition would be greatly)Tj-87 -12 TD 1.523 Tw (preferable to a continuation of the Mandate. However, Baron Van Asbeck took the )Tj386 0 TD 0 Tw (opposite)Tj-386 -12 TD 0.212 Tc (view. The Chairman, Pierre )Tj130 0 TD 0 Tc 2.426 Tw (Orts believed that partition was in harmony with the spirit of)Tj-130 -12 TD 0.407 Tc 0 Tw (the )Tj18 0 TD 0 Tc 1.772 Tw (Covenant of the League of Nations and of the Balfour Declaration and that it took into)Tj-18 -12 TD 0 Tw (account )Tj36 0 TD 2.64 Tw (the principle of the dual obligation towards both Jews and Arabs. However, Orts)Tj-36 -12 TD -0.101 Tc 0 Tw (considered )Tj50 0 TD 0 Tc 1.908 Tw (that too early an establishment of independent Arab and Jewish States might be)Tj-50 -12 TD -0.206 Tc 0 Tw (dangerous and )Tj66 0 TD 0 Tc 1.954 Tw (suggested instead the establishment of separate Mandates for the Jewish and)Tj-66 -12 TD 0.348 Tc 0 Tw (the Arab States.)Tj26 -12 TD 0.064 Tc (During the course of the Commission's sessions, the British Colonial )Tj308 0 TD 0 Tc 9.54 Tw (Secretary, William)Tj-334 -12 TD 0.049 Tc 0 Tw (Ormsby-Gore, was subjected to both an oral and a written critical examination of )Tj364 0 TD 0 Tc 6.044 Tw (the partition)Tj-364 -12 TD 0.017 Tc 0 Tw (plan, including searching questions regarding the possibly compulsory transfer of population.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 2.264 Tw (At one of the early sessions, )Tj139 0 TD 3.424 Tw (Ormsby-Gore made a general statement regarding the)Tj-165 -12 TD 2.346 Tw (proposed population transfer. He referred to the recommendation in the Peel Report )Tj404 0 TD 0.914 Tc 0 Tw (that)Tj-404 -12 TD 0 Tc (efforts be made )Tj71 0 TD 2.084 Tw (to arrange some transfer of population. "But this," said Ormsby--Gore, "will)Tj-71 -12 TD 1.532 Tw (take time, and do not let the )Tj137 0 TD 2.615 Tw (Mandates Commission imagine that transfers of population -)Tj-137 -12 TD 0.209 Tc 0 Tw (particularly of Arab cultivators )Tj147 0 TD 0 Tc 1.073 Tw (wedded for generations to their land - is going to be an easy)Tj-147 -12 TD 0.552 Tc 0 Tw (matter!")Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.464 Tw (He suggested that the "cause of a lot of )Tj187 0 TD 3.163 Tw (trouble already in Palestine" had been cases)Tj-213 -12 TD 0.18 Tc 0 Tw ("where Jews have acquired land and displaced the cultivators." \()Tj/F4 7 Tf296 3 TD (1)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-274 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.321 Tw (However, in 1931, an investigation carried out by )Tj231 0 TD 4.019 Tw (Lewis French, into allegations that)Tj-257 -12 TD 0.08 Tc 0 Tw (Jewish land purchases were leaving Arab farmers )Tj226 0 TD 0 Tc 2 Tw (landless, had found these allegations to be)Tj-226 -12 TD 0.064 Tc 0 Tw (largely groundless.)Tj26 -12 TD 0.072 Tc (Large tracts of )Tj67 0 TD 0 Tc 1.347 Tw (land in Palestine were owned by absentee landlords, frequently residing)Tj-93 -12 TD 0.226 Tc 0 Tw (in distant Arab capitals )Tj111 0 TD 0 Tc 1.755 Tw (and worked by tenant farmers who were Arab peasants \(fellaheen\).)Tj-111 -12 TD 0 Tw (Lands )Tj29 0 TD 1.272 Tw (purchased by Jews could only be registered when the Registrar of Lands was satisfied)Tj-29 -12 TD 2.084 Tw (that each tenant farmer involved would "retain sufficient land in the District or )Tj378 0 TD 0.287 Tc 0 Tw (elsewhere)Tj-378 -12 TD 0.21 Tc (for the maintenance of himself and his family." \()Tj/F4 7 Tf223 3 TD (2)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-201 -12 TD 0.205 Tc (Incidentally, the word "elsewhere" in this Ordinance )Tj245 0 TD 0 Tc 2.353 Tw (and the phrase "other land" in a)Tj-271 -12 TD 1.51 Tw (1922 Ordinance requiring compensation of tenants by cash or "other land", indicate that )Tj409 0 TD 0.709 Tc 0 Tw (the)Tj-409 -12 TD 0.118 Tc (British authorities did not disapprove of the transfer of Arab tenant farmers from one place )Tj416 0 TD 0 Tc (to)Tj-416 -12 TD 0.3 Tc (another.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 2.357 Tw (Ormsby-Gore warned the Commission that the transfer of )Tj278 0 TD 4.103 Tw (Arab families out of the)Tj-304 -12 TD 0.045 Tc 0 Tw (proposed Jewish State would be a "slow laborious process". He then )Tj306 0 TD 0 Tc 1.956 Tw (tendered the views of his)Tj-306 -12 TD 0 Tw (Government )Tj58 0 TD 3.925 Tw (regarding the possibilities of transfer "assuming the Arabs are prepared to)Tj-58 -12 TD 0.063 Tc 0 Tw (move." The Peel Report had spoken of a compulsory transfer, if necessary, for the )Tj367 0 TD 0 Tc 1.92 Tw (Arabs of the)Tj-367 -12 TD 0.279 Tc 0 Tw (Plains. Until )Tj61 0 TD 0 Tc 1.04 Tw (then, Ormsby-Gore had made no reservations in all his pronouncements on this)Tj-61 -12 TD 0.21 Tc 0 Tw (subject. This was the first time that he had )Tj198 0 TD 0 Tc 0.97 Tw (spoken of the need for agreement by the Arabs to)Tj-198 -12 TD 3.058 Tw (such a transfer. It would seem that under )Tj209 0 TD 4.904 Tw ("cross-examination" by the members of this)Tj-209 -12 TD -0.062 Tc 0 Tw (Commission, and opposition from certain quarters, including some Jewish sources, he was put )Tj415 0 TD -0.64 Tc (on)TjET0.24 w 85.12 131.88 m 228.12 131.88 l S 1 w BT/F4 7 Tf85.5 119 TD (1)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0 Tc 1.074 Tw ( / League of )Tj50 0 TD 2.212 Tw (Nations, Permanent Mandates Commission, Minutes of the Thirty-Second \(Extraordinary\) Session,)Tj-54 -9 TD 0.094 Tc 0 Tw (Geneva, July - August 1937, \(henceforth PMC Minutes\), pp.21-22.)Tj/F4 7 Tf0 -7 TD (2)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.041 Tc ( / Aumann, Land Ownership in Palestine, op. cit., p.14.)Tj/F5 7 Tf172 -31 TD 0.269 Tc (\321    215    \321)TjET
