ate in the House of Commons was concluded, the House resolving "That the)Tj-47 -12 TD 0.313 Tw (proposals contained in Command )Tj154 0 TD 2.627 Tw (Paper No. 5513 \(the Peel Commission Report\) relating to)Tj-154 -12 TD 2.617 Tw (Palestine should be )Tj96 0 TD 3.503 Tw (brought before the League of Nations with a view to enabling His)Tj-96 -12 TD 2.251 Tw (Majesty's Government after adequate inquiry, to present )Tj268 0 TD 4.486 Tw (to Parliament a definite scheme)Tj-268 -12 TD 1.717 Tw (taking into )Tj54 0 TD 2.372 Tw (full account all the recommendations of the Command Paper." \()Tj/F4 7 Tf303 3 TD (2)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD 4.866 Tw (\) The British)Tj-361 -12 TD 1.079 Tw (Parliament did not reject any of the recommendations of the Peel Commission, including )Tj409 0 TD 0.709 Tc 0 Tw (the)Tj-409 -12 TD 0.112 Tc (population transfer proposal.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.719 Tw (Ben-Gurion, however, after reading )Tj167 0 TD 3.597 Tw (reports of the Parliamentary debates was rather)Tj-193 -12 TD 1.84 Tw (pessimistic, in particular in connection with the )Tj225 0 TD 2.68 Tw (transfer proposal. In his diary entry of 22)Tj-225 -12 TD 0.231 Tc 0 Tw (July, he wrote that the )Tj106 0 TD 0 Tc 1.264 Tw (most doubtful item was the removal of the Arabs and in the event of)Tj-106 -12 TD 0.062 Tc 0 Tw (its non-implementation, it would )Tj151 0 TD 0 Tc 2.045 Tw (be just for the British to compensate the Jewish State with)Tj-151 -12 TD 0.33 Tc 0 Tw (additional territory. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf101 3 TD (3)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-79 -12 TD 0.14 Tc (The Parliamentary Debates in both Houses showed a diversity )Tj287 0 TD 0 Tc 1.266 Tw (of opinion regarding the)Tj-313 -12 TD 5.847 Tw (transfer of population. Three speakers directly supported the )Tj319 0 TD 7.072 Tw (transfer proposals as)Tj-319 -12 TD -0.18 Tc 0 Tw (recommended by the Commission. Two members in )Tj226 0 TD 0 Tc 1.836 Tw (suggesting that the Arab population of the)Tj-226 -12 TD 2.198 Tw (Galilee \(which was intended to )Tj151 0 TD 2.629 Tw (become part of the Jewish State\) should be transferred to)Tj-151 -12 TD 0 Tw (Syria \(and Lebanon\), were in favour of a more comprehensive )Tj277 0 TD 2.613 Tw (transfer than that envisaged by)Tj-277 -12 TD 0 Tw (the Peel Commission. )Tj99 0 TD 1.529 Tw (Of these five supporters of transfer, three were supporters of the Arab)Tj-99 -12 TD 1.692 Tw (cause, having realised the necessity of removing the Arab )Tj272 0 TD 2.957 Tw (population from the area of the)Tj-272 -12 TD 0.082 Tc 0 Tw (proposed Jewish State, in order to avoid future friction.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 0.526 Tw (Two members spoke strongly against transfer. Another member began by )Tj334 0 TD 1.814 Tw (opposing it in)Tj-360 -12 TD 1.64 Tw (moderate terms, but )Tj97 0 TD 3.314 Tw (during the course of his speech showed himself prepared to accept)Tj-97 -12 TD 1.275 Tw (transfer with certain provisos. One member did )Tj222 0 TD 1.621 Tw (not want to see a compulsory transfer. Two)Tj-222 -12 TD 4.557 Tw (speakers were concerned that transfer might place Arab )Tj286 0 TD 5.337 Tw (peasants under feudal Arab)Tj-286 -12 TD 3.036 Tw (landlords. A few speakers were sceptical of the ability of the )Tj302 0 TD 3.536 Tw (Government to effect this)Tj-302 -12 TD 0.089 Tc 0 Tw (transfer. Finally, many members did )Tj167 0 TD 0 Tc 1.181 Tw (not even mention the proposal to transfer population, in)Tj-167 -12 TD 0.277 Tc 0 Tw (their speeches.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.68 Tw (Following the debate in the House of Commons, "a group of Members )Tj333 0 TD 6.2 Tw (of Parliament)Tj-359 -12 TD 0.052 Tc 0 Tw (representative of responsible pro-Arab opinion )Tj213 0 TD 0 Tc 1.384 Tw (in this country" submitted a letter to Ormsby)Tj208 0 TD (-)Tj-421 -12 TD 0.819 Tw (Gore, which he in turn passed on to )Tj167 0 TD 3.947 Tw (the Cabinet. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf67 3 TD (4)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD 3.082 Tw (\) There were twelve signatories to this)Tj-238 -12 TD 1.795 Tw (letter. They included Douglas )Tj141 0 TD 2.61 Tw (Clifton Brown, Anthony Crossley and Arnold Wilson, all of)Tj-141 -12 TD 0 Tw (whom had spoken on the transfer of Arabs, in their speeches in the House of Commons.)Tj26 -12 TD 0.301 Tc (In their letter, )Tj68 0 TD 0 Tc 1.567 Tw (they wrote concerning transfer: "The principal obstacle to the successful)Tj-94 -12 TD 2.535 Tw (formation of the proposed states is the existence )Tj235 0 TD 4.372 Tw (of the large Arab minority within the)Tj-235 -12 TD 0.051 Tc 0 Tw (borders of the Jewish State. We doubt the possibility of compulsory transference; but we )Tj397 0 TD 0 Tc (do not)Tj-397 -12 TD 0.089 Tc (question the need and desirability of transference on a voluntary basis." \()Tj/F4 7 Tf329 3 TD (5)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD 0 Tc (\) As )Tj20 0 TD (we can see from)Tj-353 -12 TD 2.566 Tw (this letter, even a pro-Arab Parliamentary lobby realised )Tj272 0 TD 2.599 Tw (that it would be undesirable for)TjET0.24 w 85.12 152.88 m 228.12 152.88 l S 1 w BT/F4 7 Tf85.5 140 TD (1)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0 Tw ( / Ibid., col.2342.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (2)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD ( / Ibid., col.2367.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (3)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.019 Tc ( / David Ben-Gurion, Handwritten Diary entry 27 July 1937, \(BGA\) ; Ben-Gurion, Memoirs, vol.4, op. cit., p.318.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (4)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0 Tc ( / Ormsby-Gore to Cabinet, 11 August 1937, \(PRO CAB C.P. 203\(37\)\).)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (5)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD ( / 12 M.P.s to Ormsby-Gore, 29 July 1937, \(PRO CAB C.P. 203\(37\)\).)Tj/F5 7 Tf172 -31 TD 0.269 Tc (\321    214    \321)TjET
