vour of transferring the Arabs.)Tj26 -12 TD 0.154 Tc (In his book, "Partition of Eretz-Israel in Mandatory )Tj235 0 TD 0 Tc 1.9 Tw (Period", Shmuel Dothan, concluded)Tj-261 -12 TD 0.053 Tc 0 Tw (from the proceedings of this Conference that "this transfer question was shown )Tj356 0 TD 0 Tc 1.952 Tw (in a new light.)Tj-356 -12 TD 0.076 Tc 0 Tw (No longer did the apologists )Tj131 0 TD 0 Tc 1.518 Tw (try to prove that the Arabs had never been removed from their)Tj-131 -12 TD 2.513 Tw (land, but more honestly denied that )Tj176 0 TD 3.677 Tw (there was anything wrong with such removal. The)Tj-176 -12 TD 0.105 Tc 0 Tw (transfer of Arabs from the Jewish area to an Arab State was not a sin, but an ethical )Tj378 0 TD 0 Tc 4.471 Tw (act which)Tj-378 -12 TD 0.1 Tc 0 Tw (would benefit both Jews and Arabs alike." \()Tj/F4 7 Tf196 3 TD (3)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj/F3 10 Tf-200 -24 TD 0.181 Tc (British Parliamentary Debates)Tj/F4 10 Tf26 -12 TD 0.288 Tc (The British Parliament has two )Tj148 0 TD 0 Tc 0.89 Tw (chambers. The upper Chamber, known as the House of)Tj-174 -12 TD 3.454 Tw (Lords, in 1937 consisted mainly of hereditary )Tj226 0 TD 4.989 Tw (lords temporal and non-hereditary lords)Tj-226 -12 TD 1.232 Tw (spiritual. The lower Chamber, the House of Commons, )Tj255 0 TD 1.867 Tw (in 1937 had 640 members elected by)Tj-255 -12 TD 0.042 Tc 0 Tw (popular franchise, each member representing a specific constituency of the United Kingdom.)Tj26 -12 TD -0.201 Tc (On 20 and 21 )Tj58 0 TD 0 Tc 1.339 Tw (July 1937, both Houses of Parliament debated the recommendations of the)Tj-84 -12 TD 1.545 Tw (Peel Commission. In the Parliamentary Reports on both these debates, only )Tj352 0 TD 3.351 Tw (a few scattered)Tj-352 -12 TD 0.317 Tc 0 Tw (paragraphs dealt )Tj83 0 TD 0 Tc 1.693 Tw (with the question of the transfer of population. Many of the speakers did)Tj-83 -12 TD 0.13 Tc 0 Tw (not mention transfer at all.)Tj26 -12 TD 0.057 Tc (The debate in the House of )Tj123 0 TD 0 Tc 0.618 Tw (the Lords began on the 20 July and was adjourned to 21 July.)Tj-149 -12 TD 2.232 Tw (Early speakers in this )Tj106 0 TD 2.53 Tw (debate were Lord Peel, Chairman of the Peel Commission, and the)Tj-106 -12 TD 0 Tw (Marquess of Dufferin and Ava, who was )Tj182 0 TD 2.626 Tw (the Deputy Colonial Secretary. However, neither of)Tj-182 -12 TD 0.113 Tc 0 Tw (these speakers specifically mentioned the subject of population transfer.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.762 Tw (The )Tj21 0 TD 2.648 Tw (first speaker to mention transfer was Viscount Samuel \(Herbert Samuel\). In the)Tj-47 -12 TD 2.007 Tw (period leading up to the Balfour Declaration, Herbert Samuel, )Tj294 0 TD 2.834 Tw (a Jew, played an important)Tj-294 -12 TD 2.564 Tw (role in the preliminary behind-the-scene activities, constantly guiding )Tj331 0 TD 4.786 Tw (the Zionist leaders.)Tj-331 -12 TD 0.139 Tc 0 Tw (Weizmann described him as "discreet, tactful )Tj208 0 TD 0 Tc 2.624 Tw (and insistent." When the British were granted)Tj-208 -12 TD 2.628 Tw (the )Tj19 0 TD 2.873 Tw (Mandate over Palestine, Samuel, was appointed as the First High Commissioner and)Tj-19 -12 TD 1.36 Tw (served between the years 1920-25. During the )Tj213 0 TD 3.28 Tw (early part of his term of office, he laid the)Tj-213 -12 TD 0 Tw (foundations of the )Tj84 0 TD 2.647 Tw (Palestine civil administration and gave official recognition to the Jewish)Tj-84 -12 TD 0.172 Tc 0 Tw (representative bodies. However, later )Tj175 0 TD 0 Tc 1.641 Tw (in his term of office, he made efforts to appease Arab)Tj-175 -12 TD 0.155 Tc 0 Tw (anti-Zionism and restricted Jewish immigration into Palestine.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.021 Tw (In his speech to the House of Lords, )Tj169 0 TD 3.1 Tw (Samuel came out against the partition proposal.)Tj-195 -12 TD 2.403 Tw (This )Tj24 0 TD 2.3 Tw (caused anger not only in the Zionist community, but also in the ranks of the British)Tj-24 -12 TD 0.064 Tc 0 Tw (Government, who had gradually 