re many )Tj254 0 TD 0 Tc 1.156 Tw (difficulties to surmount to implement)Tj-254 -12 TD -0.037 Tc 0 Tw (such a plan, and that one must not run away from them )Tj247 0 TD 0 Tc 0.502 Tw (but overcome them. He thus continued,)Tj-247 -12 TD 0.179 Tc 0 Tw ("[We must] find a receptive ear principally in America, afterwards in Britain )Tj353 0 TD 0 Tc 1.759 Tw (and then in the)Tj-353 -12 TD 0 Tw (neighbouring countries..We will set up an apparatus composed of experts from the )Tj369 0 TD 2.753 Tw (Yishuv, and)Tj-369 -12 TD 0.169 Tc 0 Tw (they will supervise the [Arab] transfer )Tj178 0 TD 0 Tc 2.411 Tw (and the preparations for resettlement 'there' [outside)Tj-178 -12 TD 0.348 Tc 0 Tw (Palestine]." \()Tj/F4 7 Tf60 3 TD (3)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-38 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.345 Tw (Two weeks later, Weitz succeeded after much effort in obtaining a short meeting )Tj376 0 TD 0.667 Tc 0 Tw (with)Tj-402 -12 TD 0.199 Tc (Moshe Shertok and Eliezer Kaplan. In his diary, Weitz reported how at )Tj330 0 TD 0 Tc 2.59 Tw (this meeting he had)Tj-330 -12 TD 1.591 Tw (lectured to Shertok that "our redemption will come only if )Tj275 0 TD 2.927 Tw (the land is vacated for us". He)Tj-275 -12 TD 0.137 Tc 0 Tw (pointed out that "the transfer of the Arab population is essential" in order to solve )Tj375 0 TD 0 Tc 4.199 Tw (the Jewish)Tj-375 -12 TD 2.26 Tw (question. Weitz suggested that the Jewish Agency set up a committee )Tj334 0 TD 1.049 Tw (comprising between)Tj-334 -12 TD 2.888 Tw (three )Tj28 0 TD 3.303 Tw (to five people "to investigate the possibility of Arab settlement in Iraq, Syria and)Tj-28 -12 TD 1.291 Tw (Transjordan". Such a committee would have to make a thorough study of this )Tj362 0 TD 2.541 Tw (subject which)Tj-362 -12 TD 0.598 Tw (would be able to stand up to )Tj133 0 TD 2.106 Tw (expert scrutiny and it would have to do its work quickly and)Tj-133 -12 TD 2.697 Tw (without publicity. Both Shertok and Kaplan )Tj212 0 TD 2.894 Tw (were prepared to participate in such a plan.)Tj-212 -12 TD 0.091 Tc 0 Tw (Weitz also put forward ideas for financing such transfer. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf261 3 TD (4)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-239 -12 TD 0 Tc 4.338 Tw (Towards the end of August 1941, Weitz spoke at length with )Tj318 0 TD 8.681 Tw (Berl Katznelson.)Tj-344 -12 TD 2.039 Tw (Commenting on this meeting, Weitz )Tj173 0 TD 3.669 Tw (wrote in his diary, "On the question of 'population)Tj-173 -12 TD 2.044 Tw (transfer', it appears that he has supported this idea for many years, and )Tj344 0 TD 5.668 Tw (furthermore, like)Tj-344 -12 TD 0.042 Tc 0 Tw (me, he sees in it the only solution to our problem in Palestine." )Tj281 0 TD 0 Tc 3.928 Tw (Katznelson believed that after)Tj-281 -12 TD 0.195 Tc 0 Tw (the war, the world )Tj88 0 TD 0 Tc 1.223 Tw (leaders would support the idea of population transfer and he encouraged)Tj-88 -12 TD 2.043 Tw (Weitz to assemble material for a plan to settle )Tj222 0 TD 2.718 Tw (the Arabs of Palestine in the neighbouring)Tj-222 -12 TD 0.1 Tc 0 Tw (countries, and he promised to speak to Kaplan and Shertok about this matter. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf356 3 TD (5)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-334 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.709 Tw (On 1 September, Weitz received a visa to travel to Syria and Lebanon, the )Tj351 0 TD 0 Tw (purpose of)Tj-377 -12 TD 0.192 Tc (his visit being to look into the practical possibilities of Arab transfer. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf322 3 TD (6)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-300 -12 TD 0.219 Tc (Five days later, Weitz went to visit the kibbutz Migdal )Tj255 0 TD 0 Tc 0.625 Tw (Haemek - a kibbutz founded on)Tj-281 -12 TD 1.667 Tw (land from which Arabs had )Tj133 0 TD 1.7 Tw (been transferred. He spoke to some of the kibbutz members in)Tj-133 -12 TD 2.967 Tw (detail "on the plan for population )Tj168 0 TD 4.786 Tw (transfer". Ya'akov Hazan answered that he and his)Tj-168 -12 TD -0.098 Tc 0 Tw (kibbutz movement would oppose it because it was useless, could not )Tj301 0 TD 0 Tc 0.451 Tw (be implemented and would)Tj-301 -12 TD 0.286 Tc 0 Tw (harm relations with the )Tj113 0 TD 0 Tc 0.798 Tw (Arabs. Some members of the kibbutz bombarded him with questions)Tj-113 -12 TD 0.112 Tc 0 Tw (whose essence was that they did not believe in its )Tj227 0 TD 0 Tc 4.578 Tw (feasibility, although they liked the idea.)Tj-227 -12 TD (\()Tj/F4 7 Tf4 3 TD (7)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj18 -12 TD 0.094 Tc 0 Tw (On the following morning whilst in Haifa, he spoke to Eliahu Epstein and informed )Tj380 0 TD 0.48 Tc (him)Tj-406 -12 TD 0.063 Tc (of the reason for his journey to Syria. )Tj168 0 TD 0 Tc 2.243 Tw ("In his [Epstein's] opinion", he wrote in his diary, "the)Tj-168 -12 TD 0.169 Tc 0 Tw (plan for transfer of population is the only plan which will solve the Palestine problem." \()Tj/F4 7 Tf406 3 TD (8)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))TjET0.24 w 85.12 191.88 m 228.12 191.88 l S 1 w BT/F4 7 Tf85.5 179 TD (1)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.019 Tc ( /  Joseph Weitz, Handwritten Diary entries: 13 February 1941, p.1117, 18 March 1941, )Tj307 0 TD 0 Tc 1.362 Tw (p.1126, 20 March 1941, p.1127,)Tj-311 -9 TD 0.02 Tc 0 Tw (4 May 1941, p.1142, 13 May 1941, p.1149, 22 June 1941, p.1169, \(CZA A246/7\) ; Ibid., pp.190, 191-92, 192, 195, 202.)Tj/F4 7 Tf0 -7 TD (2)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.039 Tc ( / Joseph Weitz, Handwritten Diary entry 22 June 1941, pp.1169-70, \(CZA A246/7\) ; Ibid., p.202.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (3)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD ( / Joseph Weitz, Handwritten Diary entry 26 June 1941, pp.1172-73, \(CZA A246/7\) ; Ibid., p.203.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (4)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.03 Tc ( / Joseph Weitz, Handwritten Diary entry 10 July 1941, pp.1180-81, \(CZA A246/7\) ; Ibid., pp.205-06.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (5)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.02 Tc ( / Joseph Weitz, Handwritten Diary entry 28 August 1941, p.1207, \(CZA A246/7\) ; Ibid., p.214.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (6)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.026 Tc ( / Joseph Weitz, Handwritten Diary entry 1 September 1941, p.1209, \(CZA A246/7\) ; Ibid.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (7)Tj/