hich he gave a report of his)Tj-138 -12 TD 0.273 Tc 0 Tw (visit to Iraq. Bell reported that whilst the Iraqi ministers had said that the transfer of )Tj398 0 TD 0 Tc (Arabs)Tj-398 -12 TD 1.484 Tw (from Palestine to )Tj82 0 TD 2.519 Tw (Iraq "was out of the question at the present time, they were inclined to)Tj-82 -12 TD 1.828 Tw (nibble at the idea as a future possibility." Downie noted )Tj266 0 TD 3.172 Tw (that "Mr. Bell was careful not to)Tj-266 -12 TD 3.226 Tw (mention Mr. Norman and )Tj130 0 TD 5.822 Tw (his scheme, and tried to convey the impression that his)Tj-130 -12 TD 2.149 Tw (investigations were )Tj93 0 TD 3.033 Tw (purely personal." Bell's behaviour was in fact very understandable in)Tj-93 -12 TD 0.172 Tc 0 Tw (view of the fact that Downie had been hostile to Norman's plan.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 2.863 Tw (Bell emphasised the advantages of such a transfer, but )Tj268 0 TD 6.424 Tw (fully appreciated that the)Tj-294 -12 TD 0.062 Tc 0 Tw (scheme depended on the willingness of the Arabs of Palestine to transfer to Iraq, )Tj363 0 TD 0 Tc 3.769 Tw (which at the)Tj-363 -12 TD 0.156 Tc 0 Tw (time was most unlikely. He understood that the British )Tj254 0 TD 0 Tc 1.184 Tw (Government could not associate itself)Tj-254 -12 TD 2.143 Tw (with any )Tj46 0 TD 2.855 Tw (such scheme. Downie then referred to the difference that the transferred Arabs)Tj-46 -12 TD 0.108 Tc 0 Tw (would experience between the temperate climate of Palestine and the )Tj316 0 TD 0 Tc 3.921 Tw (excessively hot climate)Tj-316 -12 TD 0.127 Tc 0 Tw (of the lower reaches of the Tigris and Euphrates. )Tj223 0 TD 0 Tc 1.423 Tw (Towards the conclusion of the meeting, Bell)Tj-223 -12 TD 3.203 Tw (asked Downie "what )Tj104 0 TD 3.693 Tw (attitude the British Government would adopt to any proposal for)Tj-104 -12 TD 0.092 Tc 0 Tw (encouraging the voluntary immigration of Palestinian Arabs to Iraq." )Tj314 0 TD 0 Tc 1.239 Tw (Downie said he was not)Tj-314 -12 TD 0.067 Tc 0 Tw (in a position to give an official answer, but in )Tj205 0 TD 0 Tc 0.741 Tw (his own personal opinion, he saw no prospect of)Tj-205 -12 TD 0.142 Tc 0 Tw (the Arabs being prepared to leave Palestine for Iraq, or the Iraqi )Tj294 0 TD 0 Tc -1.9 Tw (government encouraging such)Tj-294 -12 TD 0.093 Tc 0 Tw (a development. However, if at a future date, these Arabs would wish to migrate and )Tj383 0 TD 0 Tc 4.798 Tw (the Iraqi)Tj-383 -12 TD -0.237 Tc 0 Tw (Government be )Tj68 0 TD 0 Tc 1.25 Tw (willing to receive them, he could "not see any reason why the Government of)Tj-68 -12 TD 0.195 Tc 0 Tw (Palestine should stand in the way." \()Tj/F4 7 Tf168 3 TD (3)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-146 -12 TD 0 Tc 3.391 Tw (In October, two of the most prominent Iraqi political )Tj265 0 TD 3.524 Tw (leaders were in London for)Tj-291 -12 TD 2.855 Tw (conferences with British officials in connection )Tj225 0 TD 4.35 Tw (with the severe disturbances which then)Tj-225 -12 TD 2.127 Tw (prevailed in Palestine. Bell undertook to utilise this opportunity to )Tj317 0 TD 4.698 Tw (strengthen his already)Tj-317 -12 TD 0.314 Tc 0 Tw (friendly relationship with them. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf158 3 TD (4)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-136 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.096 Tw (At about the same time, Norman who was in )Tj211 0 TD 3.656 Tw (the United States, had a meeting with)Tj-237 -12 TD 1.578 Tw (Cyrus Adler, the President )Tj127 0 TD 2.978 Tw (of the American Jewish Committee. Three months later, Adler)Tj-127 -12 TD -0.047 Tc 0 Tw (reported on the contents of this meeting to Ben-Gurion. According )Tj293 0 TD 0 Tc 2.014 Tw (to Ben-Gurion's diary, Adler)Tj-293 -12 TD 2.443 Tw (had offered to assist Norman with his plan and to this end, had given him a number )Tj416 0 TD 0 Tw (of)Tj-416 -12 TD 0.033 Tc (sources. Norman was of the opinion, that if one were to give an Arab from fifty )Tj355 0 TD 0 Tc (to two hundred)Tj-355 -12 TD 0.066 Tc (and fifty acres of land in Iraq in exchange for his two to )Tj251 0 TD 0 Tc 1.667 Tw (three acres in Palestine, he would go.)Tj-251 -12 TD 1.663 Tw (This was )Tj45 0 TD 2.055 Tw (the method which had been used by the American government to encourage the)Tj-45 -12 TD 0.381 Tc 0 Tw (early )Tj27 0 TD 0 Tc 1.619 Tw (settlers to migrate westwards. Adler had concluded that Norman was caught up with)Tj-27 -12 TD 2.113 Tw (his transfer idea, and )Tj104 0 TD 2.828 Tw (that he had already found supporters and that he would also find)Tj-104 -12 TD 0.231 Tc 0 Tw (additional supporters. He observed that Sieff\(?\) was also dealing with this matter. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf387 3 TD (5)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj/F3 10 Tf-391 -24 TD -0.095 Tc (Meetings with American Government Officials)Tj/F4 10 Tf26 -12 TD 0 Tc 2.807 Tw (About 17 October 1938, Norman received a long letter from )Tj293 0 TD 6.303 Tw (Montague Bell \(letter)Tj-319 -12 TD -0.118 Tc 0 Tw (untraced\) who was in London. )Tj135 0 TD 0 Tc 1.669 Tw (He had obviously asked Bell's advice on the wisdom of trying)TjET0.24 w 85.12 152.88 m 228.12 152.88 l S 1 w BT/F4 7 Tf85.5 140 TD (1)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.015 Tc 0 Tw ( / H. T. Montague Bell, "Iraq Today - Townsmen and Tribesmen", The Times, \(London\), 27 October 1938, p.13.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (2)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0 Tc ( / Parliamentary Debates, Commons, vol.341, 24 November 1938, \(London, 1938\), col.2057.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (3)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.029 Tc ( / Note of interview with Mr. Montague Bell, 13 August 1938, \(PRO FO 371/21885 E4957\).)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (4)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.03 Tc ( / Second Norman Report, op. cit., p.3,)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (5)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.024 Tc ( / David Ben-Gurion, Handwritten Diary entry 6 January 1939, \(BGA\) ; Ben-Gurion, Memoirs, vol.6, op. cit.,p.80.)Tj/F5 7 Tf172 -31 TD 0.269 Tc (\321    103    \321)TjET
