BT/F2 7 Tf181.5 781 TD 0.023 Tc 0 Tw (Chaim SIMONS : Proposals to Transfer Arabs from Palestine)Tj-10 -10 TD -0.18 Tc (\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321)Tj/F4 10 Tf-86 -25 TD 0.047 Tc (who earned his meager )Tj107 0 TD 0 Tc 1.529 Tw (living as the proprietor of a store that supplied goods to peddlers in)Tj-107 -12 TD 2.376 Tw (upstate New York during the 1850s. His son Aaron, a brilliant )Tj302 0 TD 2.958 Tw (businessman, reversed the)Tj-302 -12 TD 0.423 Tc 0 Tw (family )Tj34 0 TD 0 Tc 1.583 Tw (fortunes to become a millionaire. In 1919, the family name was "Americanised" from)Tj-34 -12 TD 2.994 Tw (Nusbaum to Norman. Aaron had two children, Edward and Ruth, and )Tj348 0 TD 5.312 Tw (they were both)Tj-348 -12 TD 0.096 Tc 0 Tw (educated in elite private schools in Western Europe. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf241 3 TD (1)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-219 -12 TD 0 Tc 0.86 Tw (Edward Norman was one of the non-Zionist members of the Jewish )Tj311 0 TD 2.092 Tw (Agency's Executive)Tj-337 -12 TD -0.018 Tc 0 Tw (Council and he urged the foundation of a roof organisation )Tj263 0 TD 0 Tc 0.902 Tw (to co-ordinate and funnel American)Tj-263 -12 TD 0.168 Tc 0 Tw (Jewish aid for Palestinian educational, cultural and social service institutions.)Tj26 -12 TD 0.064 Tc (Although Norman was a )Tj114 0 TD 0 Tc 0.918 Tw (non-Zionist member of the Jewish Agency's Executive Council,)Tj-140 -12 TD 1.749 Tw (this certainly does not mean that he was a "non-Zionist". Shertok commented )Tj364 0 TD 4.135 Tw (in his diary,)Tj-364 -12 TD 0.127 Tc 0 Tw (how he wished that there would be many Zionists like Norman. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf297 3 TD (2)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-275 -12 TD 0 Tc (In the )Tj29 0 TD 1.707 Tw (early 1930s, Norman conceived the idea of the transfer of Arabs from Palestine,)Tj-55 -12 TD 0.129 Tc 0 Tw (and he worked almost consistently on his plan for at least fifteen years.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc (In )Tj12 0 TD 2.633 Tw (Norman's plan, the destination of the transferred Arabs was to be Iraq. Iraq was)Tj-38 -12 TD 1.092 Tw (named Mesopotamia by the ancient Greeks and )Tj221 0 TD 2.255 Tw (it was known by this name to the Western)Tj-221 -12 TD 0.156 Tc 0 Tw (World until after the Peace Conference at Versailles in 1919. Until the end )Tj339 0 TD 0 Tc 2.496 Tw (of the First World)Tj-339 -12 TD 1.708 Tw (War, Iraq was part of )Tj105 0 TD 2.71 Tw (the Ottoman Empire. It was then given as a Mandated territory to)Tj-105 -12 TD 0.19 Tc 0 Tw (Great Britain, who made )Tj116 0 TD 0 Tc 2.19 Tw (Feisal its King. In 1932 Britain relinquished its Mandate, and Iraq)Tj-116 -12 TD 0.036 Tc 0 Tw (became an independent state.)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.885 Tw (In ancient )Tj50 0 TD 3.491 Tw (times, the irrigation systems enabled the country to support millions of)Tj-76 -12 TD 3.029 Tw (people. However, destruction and neglect of )Tj217 0 TD 5.146 Tw (the irrigation works throughout the ages,)Tj-217 -12 TD 0.036 Tc 0 Tw (resulted in a considerable decrease in )Tj169 0 TD 0 Tc 1.703 Tw (population. During the early part of this century, work)Tj-169 -12 TD 0 Tw (was done on the restoration )Tj125 0 TD 1.928 Tw (of the irrigation system, and whilst Norman was developing his)Tj-125 -12 TD 0.21 Tc 0 Tw (plan, )Tj26 0 TD 0 Tc 1.289 Tw (the construction of a great dam and diversion canal on the Tigris river was completed.)Tj-26 -12 TD 0.119 Tc 0 Tw (This project enabled an enormous area of the Shatt-el-Gharraf region lying between the )Tj398 0 TD 0.217 Tc (Tigris)Tj-398 -12 TD 0 Tc 2.001 Tw (and Euphrates rivers to be made available for cultivation )Tj272 0 TD 3.453 Tw (and settlement. The plan which)Tj-272 -12 TD 0.065 Tc 0 Tw (Norman was to put forward was to transfer the Arabs of Palestine to this region of Iraq. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf399 3 TD (3)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-377 -12 TD 0 Tc 0.82 Tw (A lot of information concerning this plan is to be found in various )Tj304 0 TD 5.049 Tw (archives throughout)Tj-330 -12 TD 0.141 Tc 0 Tw (the world. However, when studying the bibliography at the end of this )Tj326 0 TD 0 Tc 2.486 Tw (book, the reader will)Tj-326 -12 TD 2.216 Tw (notice that conspicuous by their absence are the "Edward Norman )Tj317 0 TD 6.7 Tw (private archives". My)Tj-317 -12 TD 0 Tw (inquiries )Tj42 0 TD 1.827 Tw (to locate such an archives have until very recently drawn a blank. It has however)Tj-42 -12 TD -0.338 Tc 0 Tw (just come )Tj41 0 TD 0 Tc 1.597 Tw (to my notice that Edward Norman's diary is now in the hands of the historian Dr.)Tj-41 -12 TD 0.344 Tc 0 Tw (Rafael Medoff.)Tj/F3 10 Tf0 -24 TD -0.145 Tc (Three Successive Versions of Norman's Plan)Tj/F4 10 Tf26 -12 TD 0 Tc 2.824 Tw (About 1930 Edward Norman "became interested )Tj234 0 TD 4.389 Tw (in some solution of the Palestine)Tj-260 -12 TD 0.058 Tc 0 Tw (problem" and he spent a great deal of time studying the question. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf299 3 TD (4)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD 0 Tc (\) )Tj6 0 TD 1.916 Tw (In March 1933 he visited)Tj-309 -12 TD 3.613 Tw (Palestine and on 11th of that month wrote a long letter to Israel Benjamin Brodie, )Tj414 0 TD 0 Tw (an)Tj-414 -12 TD 0.126 Tc (American Zionist leader who pioneered industrialisation in )Tj273 0 TD 0 Tc 2.488 Tw (Palestine, giving his impressions)Tj-273 -12 TD 0.068 Tc 0 Tw (of the country. He was very concerned with the rapidly increasing land prices )Tj352 0 TD 0 Tc 2.806 Tw (and added that)Tj-352 -12 TD 0.098 Tc 0 Tw ("many Arabs are refusing to sell, and without land the )Tj248 0 TD 0 Tc 1.856 Tw (future of the Jews here is limited." \()Tj/F4 7 Tf169 3 TD (5)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-421 -12 TD 0.17 Tc 0 Tw (From a later part )Tj80 0 TD 0 Tc 1.755 Tw (of his letter, we see that a solution involving the transfer of Arabs to Iraq)Tj-80 -12 TD 1.647 Tw (had already crossed his mind: "A properly-managed company might )Tj322 0 TD 3.757 Tw (accomplish something)Tj-322 -12 TD 0.03 Tc 0 Tw (along lines suggested by a conversation I had with Lord Glenconner, who said that King )Tj396 0 TD 0.598 Tc (Feisal)Tj-396 -12 TD 0 Tc (of Iraq )Tj32 0 TD 1.467 Tw (is very anxious to attract Arab farmers to his country, and might with proper people)Tj-32 -12 TD 0 Tw (arrange )Tj37 0 TD 1.078 Tw (to grant a large section of the unused fertile land of his country to be given to Arabs)Tj-37 -12 TD 0.119 Tc 0 Tw (from Palestine..It sounds far-fetched, but so did many things here that now are realities, and )Tj421 0 TD (I)TjET0.24 w 85.12 179.88 m 228.12 179.88 l S 1 w BT/F4 7 Tf85.5 167 TD (1)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.032 Tc ( /  Rafael Medoff, Baksheesh Diplomacy, \(Lanham,2001\), pp.3-7.)Tj/F4 7 Tf-4 -7 TD (2)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0 Tc ( / Moshe Shertok, )Tj66 0 TD 0.998 Tw (Handwritten Diary entry 4 February 1939, p.45, \(CZA S25/198/1\) ; Diaries of Sharett, vol.iv, op.)Tj-70 -9 TD 0 Tw (cit., p.16.)Tj/F4 7 Tf0 -7 TD (3)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.021 Tc ( / Edward Norman, An Approach to the Arab Question in Palestine, )Tj246 0 TD 0 Tc 0.695 Tw (Second Version, New York City, February 1937,)Tj-250 -9 TD 0 Tw (section iii para.28, \(CZA A246/29\).)Tj/F4 7 Tf0 -7 TD (4)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 2.008 Tw ( / Memorandum of Conversation, Proposal for settlement of Palestine Problem, )Tj306 0 TD 3.202 Tw (16 November 1938, p.2, \(NA)Tj-310 -9 TD 0 Tw (867N.01/1618\).)Tj/F4 7 Tf0 -7 TD (5)Tj/F4 8 Tf4 -3 TD 0.026 Tc ( / Norman to Brodie, 11 March 1933, p.6, \(CZA A251/17a\).)Tj/F5 7 Tf174 -31 TD 0.25 Tc (\321    85    \321)TjET
