BT/F2 7 Tf181.5 781 TD 0.023 Tc 0 Tw (Chaim SIMONS : Proposals to Transfer Arabs from Palestine)Tj-10 -10 TD -0.18 Tc (\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321\321)Tj/F4 10 Tf-86 -25 TD 0 Tc 3.416 Tw (colleagues and I attach the greatest importance to this question and we do )Tj374 0 TD 3.222 Tw (not delude)Tj-374 -12 TD 0.135 Tc 0 Tw (ourselves as to its difficulties. But the many concrete advantages, which the transferring )Tj403 0 TD 0 Tc (body)Tj-403 -12 TD (offers to us, to the Arabs and to the cause of peace between the two peoples and )Tj356 0 TD 2.889 Tw (the two states,)Tj-356 -12 TD 1.327 Tw (lead us to hope that the solution suggested by the )Tj235 0 TD 1.319 Tw (Peel Commission be not dismissed out of)Tj-235 -12 TD 2.293 Tw (hand, and that the instrument designed to put )Tj225 0 TD 3.359 Tw (it into effect be formed according to this)Tj-225 -12 TD -0.074 Tc 0 Tw (principle. Of course we do not propose to have recourse )Tj246 0 TD 0 Tc 0.712 Tw (to constraint or to exercise any coercion)Tj-246 -12 TD 0.108 Tc 0 Tw (whatsoever: only those who wish will be transferred and those who prefer to )Tj351 0 TD 0 Tc 4.798 Tw (stay will stay.")Tj-351 -12 TD 0.122 Tc 0 Tw (Weizmann suggested that after the creation of a Jewish State, many of the Moslems )Tj380 0 TD 0 Tc 2.29 Tw (and other)Tj-380 -12 TD 0.058 Tc 0 Tw (indigenous persons would wish to leave, in the same way as after the )Tj313 0 TD 0 Tc (conquest of the Caucasus)Tj-313 -12 TD 1.05 Tw (by Russia, many of the Moslems preferred )Tj197 0 TD 2.398 Tw (to emigrate to Turkey. He then wrote about the)Tj-197 -12 TD 0.142 Tc 0 Tw (liquidation of property and the economic life of the Jewish State following transfer. \()Tj/F4 7 Tf385 3 TD (1)Tj/F4 10 Tf4 -3 TD (\))Tj-363 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.998 Tw (One immediately notices from this letter that Weizmann is talking about a )Tj354 0 TD 0.151 Tc 0 Tw (voluntary)Tj-380 -12 TD 0 Tc 2.068 Tw (transfer of Arabs. Up to now, in his )Tj175 0 TD 4.254 Tw (letters and private memoranda, he talks about the)Tj-175 -12 TD 1.162 Tw ("crucial importance" of implementing the Peel Commission's recommendation on transfer )Tj411 0 TD 3.17 Tw (- a)Tj-411 -12 TD 0.036 Tc 0 Tw (recommendation which included compulsion, if necessary. Furthermore, in his letter of )Tj391 0 TD 0 Tc 1.168 Tw (14 July,)Tj-391 -12 TD 0.245 Tc 0 Tw (Weizmann specifically )Tj108 0 TD 0 Tc 1.399 Tw (queries the Government's intentions regarding the implementation of)Tj-108 -12 TD 7.456 Tw ("Paragraph )Tj60 0 TD 5.458 Tw (43 of Chapter xxii of the \(Peel\) Report" - the paragraph dealing with)Tj-60 -12 TD 0.157 Tc 0 Tw (compulsory transfer. Why then this change of heart?)Tj26 -12 TD 0 Tc 1.355 Tw (We can only suggest the following reasons. This letter was written to )Tj323 0 TD 3.215 Tw (the President of)Tj-349 -12 TD 1.457 Tw (the Permanent Mandates Commission, which was at )Tj245 0 TD 3.045 Tw (that time at an advanced stage of its)Tj-245 -12 TD 2.52 Tw (deliberations on )Tj79 0 TD 3.257 Tw (the Peel Commission's recommendations on Palestine. Early on in these)Tj-79 -12 TD 1.745 Tw (proceedings, the President himself had asked Ormsby-Gore to confirm "that in the )Tj387 0 TD 2.399 Tw (event of)Tj-387 -12 TD 0.084 Tc 0 Tw (the creation of the two new states, the proposed )Tj218 0 TD 0 Tc 1.49 Tw (transfer of the rural Arab populations would)Tj-218 -12 TD 2.671 Tw (only be effected if those populations freely consented." In other words, at that )Tj380 0 TD 5.776 Tw (stage the)Tj-380 -12 TD 0.064 Tc 0 Tw (President was against a compulsory transfer of population. [It seems that when he )Tj371 0 TD 0 Tc (summarised)Tj-371 -12 TD 1.76 Tw (all the evidence at the end of the sittings of this Commission, he )Tj307 0 TD 2.828 Tw (arrived at the conclusion)Tj-307 -12 TD 0.141 Tc 0 Tw (that a transfer would have to be )Tj148 0 TD 0 Tc 2.09 Tw (compulsory.] Weizmann, who was a politician with several)Tj-148 -12 TD 0.183 Tc 0 Tw (decades of experience, therefore realised that )Tj210 0 TD 0 Tc 0.958 Tw (it would not be prudent to ask the President to)Tj-210 -12 TD 0.028 Tc 0 Tw (put a compulsory transfer into effect.)Tj26 -12 TD 0.132 Tc (Another possibility is that Weizmann is not speaking only )Tj267 0 TD 0 Tc 2.237 Tw (for himself in this letter but)Tj-293 -12 TD 1.235 Tw (for "My colleagues and I". Since some of his )Tj206 0 TD 2.397 Tw (colleagues were against a compulsory transfer,)Tj-206 -12 TD 0.077 Tc 0 Tw (Weizmann spoke of a voluntary transfer.)Tj26 -12 TD 0.22 Tc (Nearly two )Tj55 0 TD 0 Tc 1.74 Tw (years later, in early 1939, the British convened a conference of Jewish and)Tj-81 -12 TD 0.15 Tc 0 Tw (Arab leaders )Tj61 0 TD 0 Tc 1.59 Tw (at St. James's Palace in London. The Arabs, however, refused to meet with the)Tj-61 -12 TD 1.706 Tw (Jews and the British were thus forced to negotiate in separate sessions with the Jewish )Tj408 0 TD 0 Tw (and)Tj-408 -12 TD 1.7 Tw (Arab leaders. However, some unofficial contacts did take place between the )Tj356 0 TD 5.284 Tw (Jewish leaders)Tj-356 -12 TD 0.107 Tc 0 Tw (and delegates from Egypt, Saudi Arabia and Iraq. )Tj227 0 TD 0 Tc 1.781 Tw (In one of his letters, Weizmann wrote that)Tj-227 -12 TD 0.103 Tc 0 Tw (during the course of these unofficial meetings with the Iraqis, he )Tj294 0 TD 0 Tc 1.04 Tw (had on several occasions put)Tj-294 -12 TD 0.024 Tc 0 Tw (forward a proposal regarding transfer and had "more than once struck a responsive )Tj374 0 TD 0 Tc 3.402 Tw (chord". He)Tj-374 -12 TD 0.213 Tc 0 Tw (added that "one of the Iraqi delegates with whom I became rather friendly indicated that )Tj413 0 TD 0.695 Tc (he)Tj-413 -12 TD -0.071 Tc (would be prepared to )Tj97 0 TD 0 Tc 1.849 Tw (take an active part in helping such a project forward." Weizmann also)Tj-97 -12 TD 0.17 Tc 0 Tw (felt that this transfer suggestion 